A1. Eye and ear entertainment. ~ TELEVISION
A10. This makes Johnny smart. ~ CANE
A14. The man who provides stenogs with their ups and downs. ~ OTIS
A15. This fellow wears a high hat but isn't considered smart. ~ DUNCE
A16. Old-time Greek racketeer a big shot. ~ ARES
A17. Chips of the old blockhead. ~ SONS
A18. A wet spot where ancient Greeks drowned their sorrows. ~ LETHE
A19. To bob the crops. ~ REAP
A20. A blast of the bugle. ~ SNEEZE
A22. This word is very common. ~ ORDINARY
A24. The number of Swedish servant girls who are not this moment contemplating leaving for a better job. ~ NONE
A26. A bitter swallow. ~ ALOE
A27. A couple of letters expressing extreme dissatisfaction. ~ NG
A29. This kind of girl's business is continually picking up and straightening out. ~ NEAT
A31. To make wealthy (obsolete). ~ ENRICH
A35. This looks well on a good looking girl. ~ ORB
A37. A veteran doughboy. ~ ASTOR
A39. What ever did become of this girl? ~ SALLY
A40. The great enemy of youth. ~ SOAP
A42. Give a manufacturer enough rope and this is what he'll make. ~ SEGAR
A44. A vegetable life. ~ SEED
A45. What hot water dunking does for tired hot dogs. ~ EASES
A47. How the place looked after the wild party had swept over it. ~ RAZED
A49. Double this and you'll know how a Scotchman's feeling. ~ SAE
A50. Gumshoes around. ~ SNEAKS
A52. The amount of money we'd like to have these days. ~ SOME
A53. A book worm's initials. ~ TS
A54. Pinchers. ~ COPS
A56. What you take before and during a parachute jump. ~ RISK
A58. Best smelling. ~ REDOLENT
A62. What dentists get down in the mouth over (pl.). ~ NERVES
A66. An old time Red. ~ ERIC
A67. Provided with loving cup handles. ~ EARED
A69. A singular, first person love in France. ~ AIME
A70. A goat that's full of hops. ~ BOCK
A71. What motormen do on their days off. ~ DRIVE
A72. Here's an odd thing for you. ~ UNIT
A73. What a little Swedish girl does if she doesn't know what's what. ~ ASKS
A74. Reno, Paris and Mexico City. ~ SEPARATORS

D1. A bull movement. ~ TOSS
D2. The kind of jacket you wear to restaurants. ~ ETON
D3. This won't give at Edinburgh University. ~ LINE
D4. To gobble the groceries in Germany. ~ ESSEN
D5. This was recently enforced and very unpopular. ~ IDLENESS
D6. Chorus girl's favorite name. ~ SUE
D7. This is never out. ~ INTO
D8. This is often in the soup (var.). ~ OCHRA
D9. Quick Watson! ~ NEEDLE
D10. Italian skyscraper (plural). ~ CARNERAS
D11. Something made by square feet. ~ AREA
D12. Not quite beer. ~ NEAR
D13. To get a load of (poetic). ~ ESPY
D21. First name of woman whose name created a storm in American letters. ~ ZONA
D23. Little nothings. ~ IONS
D25. What a Russian gobbler is. ~ EATER
D27. Bugles. ~ NOSES
D28. To broadcast a bellyache. ~ GROAN
D30. These suited the most finicky Roman. ~ TOGAS
D32. A thousand has been knocked off these miles. (Catch on?) ~ ILES
D33. This often earmarks the well dressed football player. ~ CLEAT
D34. Two-faced fellows. ~ HYDES
D36. What home stands for to a Pirate. ~ BASE
D38. African persuader. ~ RAZOR
D41. Struttin' papas. ~ PEACOCKS
D43. A knotted handkerchief. ~ REMINDER
D46. Here's looking at you in Scandinavian. ~ SKOL
D48. Dis goes wid dose. ~ DESE
D51. Gasses along at a furious rate. ~ SPEEDS
D55. This is catching. ~ SNARE
D57. Hot dog bedding. ~ KRAUT
D58. Abie the Agent's gold. ~ REBA
D59. A lovin' little Greek baby. ~ EROS
D60. A flatfooted bull (slang). ~ DICK
D61. This comes before the nose dive ~ TRIP
D63. There was a lot of truth in this, the Romans claimed. ~ VINO
D64. A big cheese produced in Turkey. ~ EMIR
D65. Net results. ~ SETS
D68. The Little Girl of the Cross Words. ~ EVA


